GroebnerBasis::buchberger implements the classical Buchberger algorithm: Initialize the basis with the input polynomial set Construct all critical pairs (S-polynomials) For each pair, compute the S-polynomial and reduce it by multi-step division against the current basis If the remainder is nonzero, add it to the basis and update the critical pairs Repeat until no new polynomials are added Finally call minimize() (remove redundancy) and auto_reduce() (mutually reduce) pub fn buchberger<D: Domain, O: MonomialOrder>( ideal: &[SparseMultivariatePolynomial<D, O>],
) -> GroebnerBasis<D, O> { GroebnerBasis::buchberger(ideal).minimize().auto_reduce()
} Buchberger suits small ideals and teaching scenarios. Production should use F4.

