DenseUnivariatePolynomial<D> stores the coefficients from the constant term to the leading coefficient in one contiguous vector: pub struct DenseUnivariatePolynomial<D: Domain> { coeffs: Vec<D::Element>, // [a0, a1, ..., an] domain: D,
} Design decisions : Trailing-zero trimming : trailing zero coefficients are removed automatically at construction; the zero polynomial is represented by the empty vector Inlined coefficient domain : the domain field is stored inside the polynomial, avoiding the need to pass it to every operation Polynomial type parameter : the D: Domain generic allows a unified implementation over the integer ring $\mathbb{Z}$, the rational field $\mathbb{Q}$, finite fields $\mathbb{F}_p$, and so on Multiplication strategies : Condition Algorithm Complexity $\min(\deg f, \deg g) + 1 < 32$ (number of coefficients) Schoolbook (term-by-term multiplication) $O(n \cdot m)$ $\min(\deg f, \deg g) + 1 \geq 32$ Karatsuba $O(n^{1.585})$ Finite field + NTT-friendly prime + $\geq 256$ coefficients (mul_ntt) Number theoretic transform $O(n \log n)$ Note : the generic mul takes one of the first two rows (Karatsuba/Schoolbook; the threshold KARATSUBA_THRESHOLD = 32 is applied to the number of coefficients, i.e. degree $\geq 31$). The NTT is enabled through DenseUnivariatePolynomial<FiniteField>::mul_ntt, which internally checks NTT_THRESHOLD = 256 (number of coefficients) and whether the prime is NTT-friendly, and otherwise falls back to Karatsuba/Schoolbook. The core idea of Karatsuba is to split the polynomials at the midpoint $m = n/2$: $$a = a_0 + a_1 x^m, \quad b = b_0 + b_1 x^m$$ The product is computed via three half-size multiplications (instead of four): $$z_0 = a_0 b_0, \quad z_2 = a_1 b_1, \quad z_1 = (a_0 + a_1)(b_0 + b_1) - z_0 - z_2$$ $$a \cdot b = z_0 + z_1 x^m + z_2 x^{2m}$$ EuclideanDomain methods (available when $D: \text{EuclideanDomain}$): div_rem(&self, divisor) — division with remainder, returns Option<(quotient, remainder)> gcd(&self, other) — polynomial GCD (via a pseudo-remainder PRS chain) extended_gcd_poly(&self, other) — extended GCD, returns $(g, s, t)$ with $s \cdot f + t \cdot g = \text{gcd}(f, g)$ content() — the content (GCD of all coefficients) primitive_part() — the primitive part $f / \text{content}(f)$ square_free_factorization() — square-free factorization resultant(&self, other) — the resultant (Brown's PRS algorithm) pow(n) — fast exponentiation (repeated squaring) p_adic_expansion(p) — the $p$-adic expansion Univariate polynomial evaluation uses Horner's rule, in $O(n)$ time: $$f(x) = a_0 + x(a_1 + x(a_2 + \cdots + x \cdot a_n))$$ // Horner's method
let mut result = domain.zero();
for coeff in self.coeffs.iter().rev() { result = domain.mul(&result, x); result = domain.add(&result, coeff);
}

