Gebauer–Moeller Critical Pair Management The update_pairs function performs Gebauer–Moeller filtering each time a new polynomial is added to the basis: Generate candidates : pair the new polynomial with every existing element of the basis First criterion (monomial_are_coprime): if the leading monomials are coprime, skip directly (the product criterion) Second criterion (chain criterion, sequential elimination): if the $\text{lcm}$ of another (still live) candidate pair divides the current pair's $\text{lcm}$ (divisibility includes equality; duplicate pairs are dropped), the current pair is redundant and not kept Update criterion (for old pairs): an old pair is removed only when the new leading monomial divides its $\text{lcm}$ and both $\text{lcm}(i, \text{new})$ and $\text{lcm}(j, \text{new})$ are strictly smaller than $\text{lcm}(i,j)$; equality must be kept (otherwise S-polynomials required for completeness would be dropped) The CriticalPair struct precomputes the $\text{lcm}$ and the monomial differences (the information needed to construct S-polynomials), avoiding repeated computation in later steps. DivisorIndex: O(1)-ish Reducer Lookup The core operation of symbolic preprocessing is "find the element of the basis whose leading monomial divides a given monomial". A naive implementation requires a linear scan costing $O(\text{monomials} \times |\text{basis}|)$. oCAS accelerates this operation with a support bitmask index. support_mask : for an exponent vector $\alpha = (\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_n)$, define the bitmask $$\text{mask}(\alpha) = \sum_{v : \alpha_v > 0} 2^v$$ i.e. bit $v$ is set if and only if the variable $x_v$ occurs in the monomial (variable indices up to 63). Key observation : if $\text{lm}(g)$ divides $x^\alpha$, then $\text{support}(\text{lm}(g)) \subseteq \text{support}(x^\alpha)$. Hence a query only needs to enumerate the submasks of $\text{mask}(x^\alpha)$ and inspect the basis elements in the corresponding buckets. The DivisorIndex structure : struct DivisorIndex { // support_mask → list of basis element indices having this mask buckets: HashMap<u64, Vec<usize>>,
} The query find_reducer(index, basis, exp): compute $m = \text{mask}(\text{exp})$ enumerate all submasks $s \subseteq m$ of $m$ for each basis element $g$ in buckets[s], check exactly whether $\text{lm}(g) \mid x^\text{exp}$ among all reducers satisfying the condition, choose the one with the fewest terms (to speed up reduction) Enumerating submasks costs $O(3^v)$ ($v$ = the number of variables), but in practice $v$ is usually small ($\leq 10$) and the buckets contain very few elements. SimpCache: A Cache for Multiples of Reducers During symbolic preprocessing, the same basis element may be used to reduce several different monomials with different differences. SimpCache<P> caches the computed polynomial multiples: type SimpCache<P> = Vec<(SmallVec<[usize; 4]>, P)>; get_simplified(cache, diff, basis_poly) checks the cache first and returns the cached value on a hit; otherwise it computes $x^{\text{diff}} \cdot \text{basis_poly}$ and stores it in the cache. The cache persists across rounds. The ℤ_p Native Fast Path When the coefficient domain is FiniteField(p) ($p < 2^{31}$), F4 uses FpPoly instead of the generic polynomial type: struct FpPoly { terms: Vec<(i64, SmallVec<[usize; 4]>)>, // (coeff, exponent) // coeff ∈ [0, p), sorted in descending order
} All matrix operations run on i64 — no BigInt overhead. sub_scaled_fp implements O(nnz) sparse row subtraction with a two-pointer merge : fn sub_scaled_fp( row: &mut Vec<(i64, usize)>, // ascending column order pivot: &[(i64, usize)], // ascending column order c: i64, // multiplier p: i64, // modulus scratch: &mut Vec<(i64, usize)>, // scratch buffer
) { // two-pointer merge: row -= c * pivot // the first column (pivot column) is eliminated automatically (c = row[head_col] / pivot[head_col]) // all results taken mod p; zero coefficients dropped
} register_row_fp additionally caches the FpPoly representations of S-polynomials and reducer multiples for reuse across rounds. The Matrix Elimination Flow The complete F4 flow: Select critical pairs : take pairs from the queue sorted by $\text{lcm}$ size Symbolic preprocessing : expand the S-polynomial of each pair and all reducers, and build the column map Row echelonization : echelonize_fp (ℤ_p fast path) or echelonize_generic (generic) Extract new elements : convert the nonzero rows back to polynomials and check whether the leading term is "new" Update basis and pairs : add the new elements to the basis, and let update_pairs update the critical pair queue

