The Signature Structure struct Signature { module_pos: usize, // generator index k monomial: SmallVec<[usize; 4]>, // monomial exponents
} Signatures are compared with the pot (position-over-term) order: first compare module_pos (smaller first), then compare monomial with the monomial order O. LabeledPoly: Basis Elements with Signatures struct LabeledPoly<D: Domain, O: MonomialOrder> { poly: SparseMultivariatePolynomial<D, O>, sig: Signature,
} Every basis element carries a signature. When reducing with a multiple $x^\delta g$ of $g$, the signature of the new polynomial is $x^\delta \cdot \text{sig}(g)$. SyzygySet: Tracking Reductions to Zero struct SyzygySet { // module_pos → list of leading monomials of the known syzygies at that position lms: HashMap<usize, Vec<SmallVec<[usize; 4]>>>,
} insert(sig): when a matrix row reduces to zero, its signature $(k, t)$ is recorded — $t$ is stored in lms[k]. contains(sig): test a signature $(k, t)$ — if there exists $s$ in lms[k] with $s \mid t$, it is a syzygy. monomial_divides(t, s) performs the componentwise comparison ($t_i \geq s_i$ for all $i$). Matrix Construction and Elimination The build_and_reduce flow of F5: Select pairs : critical pair selection similar to F4 (shares the update_pairs implementation) Syzygy filtering : before a pair enters the matrix, check its signature — if it is a syzygy, skip it Labeled rows : each row carries (Signature, Vec<(coefficient, column)>) Symbolic preprocessing : the same column-map construction as in F4, but the signatures of reducers must propagate Row echelonization : sort the rows in ascending signature order and run Gaussian elimination Extract new elements : nonzero rows are extracted as LabeledPoly, keeping their signatures Syzygy updates : the signatures of zero rows are added to SyzygySet The ℤ_p Fast Path F5 reuses F4's infrastructure — FpPoly, CriticalPair, update_pairs — and adds signature labels via LabeledFpPoly and LabeledFpRow. The structure of f5_fp is completely symmetric to the generic f5 — only the polynomial representation switches from a generic domain to i64 modular arithmetic.

